WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.599
TLS readiness begins with endpoint discovery.

00:00:02.599 --> 00:00:05.514
Public websites are only the visible part.

00:00:05.514 --> 00:00:09.679
Organizations also run APIs, internal services, load balancers, ingress controllers,

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:13.844
reverse proxies, API gateways, service meshes, mobile backends, partner integrations,

00:00:13.844 --> 00:00:17.593
administrative consoles, monitoring endpoints, developer portals, and cloud-managed services.

00:00:17.593 --> 00:00:21.758
Each endpoint may have different owners, different clients, different libraries,

00:00:21.758 --> 00:00:23.840
and different tolerance for change.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.422
A useful inventory records the certificate, hostname, service owner, data classification,

00:00:28.422 --> 00:00:32.587
exposure, client population, TLS library, runtime version, platform, proxy path,

00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:37.168
cloud provider, CDN provider, hardware security module use, key management system,

00:00:37.168 --> 00:00:40.084
renewal process, and dependency on third-party products.

00:00:40.084 --> 00:00:42.166
That sounds like a lot,

00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:47.164
but without this context a team cannot tell which systems are easy

00:00:47.164 --> 00:00:51.746
pilots and which systems require vendor coordination or long client-update timelines.

00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:53.828
Discovery should combine multiple sources.

00:00:53.828 --> 00:00:56.327
External scanning can identify public endpoints.

00:00:56.327 --> 00:01:00.076
Configuration management can reveal load balancers and ingress rules.

00:01:00.076 --> 00:01:02.991
Certificate management systems can expose issuance patterns.

00:01:02.991 --> 00:01:05.907
Cloud inventories can show managed TLS services.

00:01:05.907 --> 00:01:09.239
Service mesh configuration can reveal internal traffic paths.

00:01:09.239 --> 00:01:14.237
Application teams can explain clients and data flows that automated tools miss.

00:01:14.237 --> 00:01:18.402
The goal is a living inventory, not a one-time screenshot.

00:01:18.402 --> 00:01:24.233
Cryptographic inventory is the foundation for PQC readiness because it connects risk to action.

00:01:24.233 --> 00:01:28.398
If a high-value API depends on an old TLS library,

00:01:28.398 --> 00:01:31.313
the next step may be runtime modernization.

00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:35.062
If a mobile app pins certificates or restricts algorithms,

00:01:35.062 --> 00:01:38.394
the next step may be client compatibility testing.

00:01:38.394 --> 00:01:41.726
If a CDN terminates TLS, vendor readiness matters.

00:01:41.726 --> 00:01:47.140
Inventory turns abstract migration into a list of owners, dependencies, tests, and decisions.
